page(CH('CHAPTER 1\nINTRODUCTION'))
S += [H1('1.1 PROBLEM STATEMENT'), P('Digital certificates and official documents are increasingly exchanged through online systems. A verifier may need to confirm that a document was issued by a trusted authority and has not been altered, but conventional workflows often require the holder to upload or share the entire document. This exposes personal information beyond the immediate purpose of verification.'), P('The project addresses the combined problem of document tamper detection, issuer authenticity and selective disclosure. A secure solution must preserve the relationship between a document, credential, issuer key and verification result. It must also provide understandable workflows for users who are not cryptography specialists.'), P('A hash can identify content but does not prove who produced it. A digital signature can establish authenticity but must be calculated over a stable canonical representation. A credential may contain sensitive attributes that should not be copied into a verifier database. The platform therefore treats document integrity, credential authenticity and private predicate verification as related but distinct operations.'), P('The backend must enforce role boundaries, prevent unauthorized object access, validate uploads, limit expensive cryptographic work and record operational events. The frontend must communicate success and failure states without presenting a proof result as stronger than what was actually checked.'), P('The problem is therefore both technical and operational: build a maintainable full-stack application that connects cryptographic primitives to a secure document workflow at the scale appropriate for an academic project and controlled pilot.'), H1('1.2 PROJECT OBJECTIVES'), P('The first objective is to register documents using streaming SHA-256 digests, validated content types and controlled artifact storage. The digest becomes a stable integrity anchor for later verification.'), P('The second objective is to issue credentials using canonical encodings and RSA-PSS signatures. The credential lifecycle must support active, suspended, revoked and expired states so that a valid signature is not treated as permanent authorization.'), P('The third objective is to support Pedersen commitments and Bulletproof range proofs for documented threshold predicates. The holder should be able to prove a statement about a private attribute without disclosing the raw value.'), P('The fourth objective is to implement role-based access for issuers, holders, verifiers and administrators, supported by JWT validation, object ownership checks, rate limiting, audit events and secure error handling.'), P('The fifth objective is to provide public verification through opaque, expiring capability tokens and QR codes. The public path must expose only the information needed to confirm validity and must resist uncontrolled repeated requests.'), P('The sixth objective is to package the system with a practical local and containerized deployment path. SQLite is retained for local demonstration, while PostgreSQL is the intended production database. Docker hardening and CI supply-chain checks provide a repeatable engineering baseline.'), P('The project does not claim a general-purpose zero-knowledge language, hardware security module integration or hyperscale deployment. Those boundaries keep the implementation understandable and maintainable.'), H1('1.3 SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT GOAL (SDG) ALIGNMENT'), P('The project aligns primarily with Sustainable Development Goal 9, which promotes resilient infrastructure, inclusive and sustainable industrialization and innovation. Digital credentials are part of modern educational, employment and public-service infrastructure, so improving their integrity and privacy supports reliable digital services.'), P('The platform contributes through technological upgrading: it combines established document-signing practices with privacy-preserving proofs. Instead of requiring every verifier to retain complete documents, a verifier can validate a bounded statement against a cryptographic anchor.'), P('The alignment is an application-level contribution rather than a claim that the project independently satisfies an SDG target. Its value lies in demonstrating how secure and privacy-aware software can be built with accessible open technologies.'), P('The system also supports responsible handling of personal information. Selective disclosure reduces unnecessary replication of attributes, while audit records provide a basis for accountability. These benefits depend on correct key management, access control, retention policy and operational discipline.'), P('The project demonstrates innovation in the combination of Java modular design, cryptographic primitives, a browser workflow and deployment controls. The implementation is intentionally sized for an academic team so that the security boundary can be understood and tested rather than hidden behind excessive infrastructure.'), Box(5.7*inch,2.2*inch,'FIGURE 1.1 - PROJECT CONTEXT DIAGRAM'), P('Figure 1.1  Project context diagram')]
br()

# Chapter 2, pages 15-21
page(CH('CHAPTER 2\nLITERATURE REVIEW'), H1('2.1 JAVA PROGRAMMING LANGUAGE'), P('Java is used as the backend language because it provides a mature runtime, strong type checking, extensive security libraries and a large ecosystem for web applications. Java 21 supplies the long-term-support runtime used by the project and provides predictable deployment characteristics for a Spring Boot service.'), P('The project benefits from Java records for immutable request and result types, standard cryptographic APIs for hashing and signatures, checked integration boundaries and a test ecosystem that supports unit, integration and adversarial testing.'), P('The use of Java also supports the academic objective of applying object-oriented design. Domain concepts such as Document, Credential, Key, Commitment and Verification Result are represented separately from HTTP controllers and storage adapters.'))
page(P('Spring Boot supplies dependency injection, HTTP routing, validation, actuator health endpoints and integration with Spring Security. The project uses JDBC and explicit stores instead of hiding all persistence behind a large object-relational abstraction. This keeps SQL and migration behavior visible for review.'), P('Maven provides the reactor build across the cryptographic, document, credential, ZKP, keys and API modules. The frontend is built through the project build process and its compiled assets are served by the API artifact.'), P('Java is not used to imply that the application is automatically secure. The runtime still requires safe configuration, dependency maintenance, key custody, request limits and tests that run against the same database dialect used in production.'))
page(H1('2.2 LITERATURE REVIEW'), P('Traditional document verification relies on issuer databases, serial numbers, signatures or visual inspection. These methods can establish document origin and integrity, but they often encourage full-document disclosure. Verifiable credential models separate the issuer, holder and verifier and create a basis for selective presentations.'), P('A cryptographic hash provides a compact representation of content. If a later digest differs, the bytes have changed. A signature binds a canonical representation to an issuer key. The verifier must still resolve the correct key, check its lifecycle and confirm that the credential has not been revoked.'), P('Zero-knowledge proofs extend this approach by allowing a prover to demonstrate a statement while keeping the witness private. In this project the proof statement is intentionally constrained to the supported predicate model.'))
page(P('Pedersen commitments are used for hiding and binding numerical attributes. The commitment combines a value with a random blinding factor. Without the blinding factor, the verifier should not learn the value from the commitment alone, subject to the security assumptions of the selected group and implementation.'), P('Bulletproof range proofs provide a non-interactive proof that a committed value lies within an allowed range. The platform uses the proof engine through a domain service so that controllers do not construct curve points or manipulate witness material directly.'), P('The literature also emphasizes that cryptographic correctness is only one part of system security. Authorization, key lifecycle, replay prevention, secure storage, error handling and operational monitoring determine whether the surrounding application preserves the intended guarantees.'))
page(P('Privacy engineering principles support data minimization, purpose limitation and controlled retention. The public verification flow therefore uses opaque capability tokens rather than placing private attributes in QR payloads. The holder flow separates credential storage from verifier presentation.'), P('Security engineering guidance recommends layered controls. The project applies input validation, size limits, CSP, frame denial, rate limiting, container hardening, secret scanning and dependency scanning. These controls reduce common application risks but do not replace a production identity provider or managed key custody.'), P('The project documentation also distinguishes local convenience from production needs: SQLite is useful for offline demonstrations, whereas PostgreSQL is expected for concurrent multi-user deployment.'))
page(P('From the reviewed concepts, the project adopts a modular monolith rather than microservices. The expected traffic and solo-team maintenance capacity do not justify distributed deployment. A single deployable service makes transaction boundaries, cryptographic context and operational debugging simpler.'), P('The technology choices are therefore consistent with the project scope: Java 21 and Spring Boot for a structured backend, React and TypeScript for role-oriented UI, PostgreSQL for production persistence, cryptographic libraries for proofs and signatures, and Docker and GitHub Actions for repeatable delivery.'), Box(5.7*inch,2.0*inch,'FIGURE 2.1 - TECHNOLOGY RELATIONSHIP DIAGRAM'), P('Figure 2.1  Technology relationship diagram'))
page(P('The review identifies a practical gap between a cryptographic demonstration and an operational platform. A proof can be mathematically valid while the wrong credential, issuer or lifecycle record is selected. Therefore, this project treats proof verification as one result in a larger trust decision that also checks identity, ownership, status, expiry and audit context.'), P('This design approach prevents the system from presenting zero-knowledge technology as a replacement for ordinary software engineering. The project is strongest when the cryptographic boundary is narrow, explicit and tested, and when the surrounding data model remains easy to inspect.'), P('The remaining chapters apply these principles to the repository implementation.'))

# Chapter 3, pages 22-29
page(CH('CHAPTER 3\nSYSTEM DESIGN'), H1('3.1 OVERALL SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE'), P('The platform is designed as a modular monolith. A React and TypeScript single-page application communicates with a Spring Boot API. The API contains security filters, controllers, application services, stores, audit services, rate-limiting services and cryptographic integration. PostgreSQL stores structured data in production; SQLite supports local operation.'), P('The deployment boundary is intentionally simple. A TLS edge should terminate public HTTPS, the API should run as a non-root container, and PostgreSQL should remain on an internal network. Document artifacts are written through an abstraction so that local filesystem storage can later be replaced by durable object storage without changing document-domain logic.'), Box(5.7*inch,2.25*inch,'FIGURE 3.1 - OVERALL SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE'), P('Figure 3.1  Overall system architecture'))
page(H1('3.1 OVERALL SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE'), P('The issuer path starts with an authenticated multipart upload. The server validates size and content format, streams the digest and creates document metadata. Issuance then creates a credential, canonicalizes its representation, signs it and persists lifecycle information.'), P('The holder path retrieves credentials associated with the holder identity, selects a supported predicate and obtains the proving material required by the ZKP service. The proof is generated under a bounded concurrency policy because it is CPU-intensive.'), P('The verifier path checks a presented proof against a credential commitment and trusted verification context. Public verification uses a capability token and returns a controlled result without returning raw hidden attributes.'))
page(H1('3.1 OVERALL SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE'), P('The administrator path manages signing-key registry records, lifecycle transitions, trust resolution and audit-chain inspection. The security design records authentication failures, denied access and relevant lifecycle events with correlation identifiers.'), P('The architecture contains several trust boundaries: browser to API, API to identity token decoder, API to database, API to artifact storage, API to signing key provider and public verifier to capability endpoint. Each boundary has a different failure mode and must be monitored separately.'), P('The design does not require microservices, event buses or Kubernetes for the current operating envelope. Those choices would increase deployment and debugging cost without solving the immediate correctness gaps.'))
page(H1('3.2 MODULE EXPLANATION'), P('The <b>zkdoc-core-crypto</b> module contains low-level digest and RSA-PSS services. It deliberately has no Spring dependency, which allows cryptographic behavior to be tested independently and reduces framework coupling.'), P('The <b>zkdoc-document</b> module models document identifiers, document records, artifact metadata, verification results, upload services and storage interfaces. It also contains filesystem and encrypted artifact storage implementations plus content validation.'), P('The <b>zkdoc-credential</b> module manages issuer records, credential lifecycle, holder credentials, canonical encoding and credential persistence. It represents the credential domain separately from transport DTOs.'))
page(H1('3.2 MODULE EXPLANATION'), P('The <b>zkdoc-zkp</b> module contains commitments, blinding-factor sources, holder proving material, predicate proof generation and statement verification. The public API exposes typed statements and results rather than curve-library details.'), P('The <b>zkdoc-keys</b> module manages registered keys, purposes, owners, lifecycle status, fingerprints, trust resolution and signing-key providers. This module is central to the distinction between development keys and production keys.'), P('The <b>zkdoc-api</b> module assembles the application. It contains controllers for documents, credentials, public verification, QR capabilities, holder workflows, predicate verification and administrative operations.'))
page(H1('3.2 MODULE EXPLANATION'), P('The frontend is organized into application routing, shared components, authentication guards, API clients, typed API models, issuer workflows, verifier workflows, holder workflows and administration screens. The route structure separates public verification from authenticated role workspaces.'), P('The module boundaries create a useful dependency direction: controllers depend on application services, services depend on domain ports, and infrastructure adapters implement storage and key interfaces. The most important design risk is persistence compatibility code that spans multiple schema generations.'), P('The architecture is maintainable for the current project, provided that the database is consolidated before the platform is used by multiple institutions.'))
page(H1('3.2 MODULE EXPLANATION'), P('The principal data entities are documents, document artifacts, credentials, credential attributes, attribute commitments, holder credentials, verification capabilities, registered keys, lifecycle events and security audit events.'), P('Document identity is separated from a physical artifact so that content versions and storage keys can be managed explicitly. Credential lifecycle records separate current status from the history of status transitions. Capability records separate the public token from the document or credential it authorizes.'), Box(5.7*inch,2.15*inch,'FIGURE 3.2 - DOMAIN ENTITY RELATIONSHIPS'), P('Figure 3.2  Domain entity relationships'))
page(H1('3.2 MODULE EXPLANATION'), P('The frontend and backend exchange JSON DTOs for structured operations and multipart requests for document uploads. API error responses include status, message, path, timestamp and correlation information, while raw stack traces and sensitive cryptographic inputs must remain server-side.'), P('The API client adds a correlation identifier to requests and attaches the current bearer token when present. Session changes are tracked so that an asynchronous response from an old session is discarded rather than applied to the active user context.'), P('This design supports clear user feedback while preserving the security boundary between browser presentation and server-side trust decisions.'))
page(H1('3.2 MODULE EXPLANATION'), P('The main system risks are configuration drift, database dialect divergence, local-only rate limiting, filesystem artifact durability and development key custody. These risks are architectural because they influence how the system behaves when deployed, not just how individual functions execute.'), P('The design response is incremental hardening: keep the modular monolith, standardize PostgreSQL for integration tests, make production profiles fail closed, restore relational constraints, move durable artifacts to object storage when replicas are introduced, and add a real TLS and identity-provider boundary.'), P('The result is an architecture that remains simple for the current team while creating explicit seams for later scale.'))
page(H1('3.2 MODULE EXPLANATION'), P('The system is designed to produce evidence for each verification decision. A successful result should identify the verified capability, document digest status, credential signature status, issuer trust status, lifecycle status and predicate outcome separately.'), P('A failed result should distinguish an expired token, revoked credential, invalid signature, digest mismatch, malformed presentation, unsupported predicate and infrastructure error. This prevents a generic green or red label from hiding what was actually established.'), P('The architecture therefore treats verification as a composed decision rather than a single cryptographic boolean.'))

# Chapter 4, pages 30-46
page(CH('CHAPTER 4\nIMPLEMENTATION'), H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('The root Maven project declares Java 21 and six modules: zkdoc-core-crypto, zkdoc-document, zkdoc-credential, zkdoc-zkp, zkdoc-keys and zkdoc-api. The API module packages the application and serves the compiled frontend assets.'), P('The source tree follows the domain boundaries described in Chapter 3. Each module has main Java sources and focused tests. The repository also contains Flyway migrations, Docker configuration, demo scripts, security documentation and frontend source.'), P('The implementation is therefore a complete project repository rather than a collection of isolated cryptographic examples.'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('The digest implementation uses streaming input rather than reading arbitrary files into a single unbounded byte array. This is important because document size limits are enforced at the HTTP boundary, but streaming still reduces memory pressure and makes the integrity calculation explicit.'), P('The RSA-PSS service defines the signature algorithm and verifies signatures using the configured public key. The canonical credential encoder ensures that the bytes signed by the issuer are the bytes reconstructed by the verifier.'), P('The tests cover valid signatures, modified content, wrong keys and malformed inputs.'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('Document upload services combine request validation, document identity creation, digesting, artifact storage and metadata persistence. The artifact store interface keeps storage concerns out of the document domain.'), P('Filesystem storage protects the base directory and derives safe storage keys. Encrypted storage wraps the artifact stream using authenticated encryption and keeps the encryption key outside the document content. The production configuration requires encryption to be enabled when the production validator is active.'), P('A production deployment should additionally verify that artifact backup and database backup are restored together.'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('Credential issuance creates a signed canonical representation and stores the credential with its issuer and lifecycle status. Lifecycle transitions are recorded as events so that a verifier can distinguish an active credential from one that was suspended or revoked later.'), P('The holder credential service associates a holder identity with an issued credential. It controls access to proving material and prevents a holder from generating a proof for a credential that is not assigned to that holder.'), P('The source structure makes the issuer, holder and verifier responsibilities visible instead of placing them in one controller.'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('Commitment creation uses a secure blinding-factor source. The commitment store persists the commitment point and attestation metadata needed to bind it to the credential. The proof service receives a typed predicate statement and private witness rather than an arbitrary JSON expression.'), P('The predicate engine is intentionally narrow. It supports the documented predicate operators and returns a proof artifact or verification result. The service does not expose the raw value to the verifier endpoint.'), Box(5.7*inch,2.0*inch,'FIGURE 4.1 - COMMITMENT AND PROOF FLOW'), P('Figure 4.1  Commitment and proof flow'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('The JWT filter extracts a bearer token, delegates decoding to a managed decoder provider, converts claims into roles and permissions, and creates an authenticated principal without retaining the raw token as credentials.'), P('The security configuration uses stateless sessions and applies route-level role checks for issuer, holder, verifier and admin endpoints. Object-level authorization services provide an additional check for ownership-sensitive operations.'), P('The configuration also installs CSP, frame denial, content-type protection and a strict referrer policy.'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('The public verification controller is intentionally unauthenticated for its public route family, but it is protected by opaque capability tokens, expiry, status checks and rate limiting. Public results should avoid returning private credential attributes or storage paths.'), P('QR capability services generate secure tokens and render QR codes that contain a controlled public URL. QR generation is a presentation convenience; the security decision remains on the server.'), P('The browser verifier supports camera scanning and manual token entry so that camera permission failure does not block the workflow.'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('Rate limiting is implemented as endpoint-aware protection. Public verification, proof verification, proof generation and other sensitive operations can use separate policies. ZKP generation also uses bounded concurrency so that a small server cannot be consumed by simultaneous expensive requests.'), P('The current limiter is process-local. That is adequate for a single pilot instance but does not provide a global quota across replicas. A later deployment with multiple instances should enforce edge or shared-store quotas while retaining local CPU guards.'), P('Every rejection should be observable through metrics and audit events without logging bearer tokens, witnesses or document content.'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('The database implementation uses JDBC stores and Flyway migrations. The local migration tree targets SQLite and the production tree targets PostgreSQL. The production deployment must use PostgreSQL because its concurrency, constraints and operational tooling match the multi-user requirement.'), P('The schema includes lifecycle, ownership, capability and security-audit concepts. Indexes support status, owner, token, expiry, credential and audit lookup operations.'), P('The migration history also contains compatibility columns and dropped foreign keys. This is an implementation area requiring consolidation before production because database constraints should enforce the domain rather than be bypassed to accommodate incompatible stores.'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('The security audit service records authentication failures, access denials, lifecycle actions and other security-relevant events. The audit chain uses sequential event information and hashes to make accidental or unauthorized modification detectable.'), P('Hash chaining improves evidence integrity but does not make a database administrator unable to rewrite history. Production audit storage should therefore include restricted database access, export or append-only retention, monitoring and a documented incident response process.'), P('Correlation IDs connect HTTP requests, logs, metrics and audit events without exposing sensitive payloads.'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('The frontend application uses React routing with public pages for home, login and verification, and protected routes for holder, issuer, audit and administrator workspaces. RequireAuth and RequireRole components prevent unauthorized navigation at the UI layer.'), P('UI guards are not the security boundary; the backend repeats authentication and authorization checks. The frontend provides loading, error and forbidden states so that an API denial is communicated clearly rather than appearing as a blank page.'), P('The shared API client adds correlation IDs, bearer authorization, JSON serialization and normalized error handling.'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('The issuer interface supports document upload and credential operations. The verifier interface supports public verification and history. The holder interface supports credential inspection and predicate proof generation. The administrator interface supports key and audit operations.'), P('A professional user journey should distinguish document integrity, signature validity, issuer trust, lifecycle state and predicate result. The frontend should not collapse these outcomes into one icon because the proof can be valid while the credential is revoked or the issuer key is untrusted.'), Box(5.7*inch,2.0*inch,'FIGURE 4.2 - FRONTEND ROLE WORKSPACES'), P('Figure 4.2  Frontend role workspaces'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('The Dockerfile uses a multi-stage build. The builder compiles the Java application and frontend, while the runtime uses an Eclipse Temurin Java 21 JRE image. The runtime user is non-root, the container exposes port 8080 and a health check calls the Spring readiness endpoint.'), P('Docker Compose provides PostgreSQL and the API on separated networks. The backend network is internal, and the API uses a persistent artifact volume. The current topology is appropriate for one host but needs an external TLS edge and a durable artifact strategy before public production.'), P('Container limits, read-only filesystems, dropped capabilities and no-new-privileges reduce the impact of a compromised process.'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('The GitHub Actions workflow checks out source, sets up Java and Node, runs backend and frontend checks, builds artifacts, scans secrets and dependencies, generates SBOMs, lints the Dockerfile and scans the built image.'), P('Pinned action SHAs improve supply-chain reproducibility. The remaining deployment gap is an end-to-end production acceptance job that starts PostgreSQL, applies migrations, runs authenticated workflows, checks response headers, performs a backup and verifies restore.'), P('The release artifact should always be tied to a commit SHA and a recorded test result.'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('Environment configuration separates local SQLite from PostgreSQL profiles and exposes storage, database, server and security properties through environment variables. Production validators reject empty database credentials, missing storage encryption material and development signing keys when the protected production profiles are used.'), P('Profile naming must be consistent. A generic postgres profile should not accidentally receive demo defaults if it is used for staging or production. The safest rule is an explicit demo profile and a fail-closed default for every non-demo profile.'), P('Secrets should be supplied by a deployment secret mechanism rather than committed .env files or image layers.'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('The API request model uses validation annotations and bounded multipart settings. File validation checks declared content against actual bytes and prevents path traversal. These controls protect application correctness, but they do not provide malware scanning or guarantee that a document is safe for downstream viewers.'), P('The verifier must treat untrusted files and tokens as input. Error responses should be sanitized, response bodies should not reflect arbitrary input without encoding, and logs should redact sensitive headers, document names and proof material where necessary.'), P('Rate limits should apply before expensive parsing or cryptographic verification whenever possible.'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('The implementation includes health and readiness probes through Spring Actuator. Readiness should reflect the ability to access the database and required key or storage dependencies, while liveness should avoid restarting the application for a recoverable downstream outage.'), P('Metrics cover authentication failures, access denials, proof generation, proof verification and rate-limit blocks. For operations, dashboards should include request latency, error rate, database pool saturation, artifact failures, proof concurrency and audit append failures.'), P('The current project is well positioned for a controlled pilot once these operational checks are connected to real alerts.'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('The implementation demonstrates object-oriented decomposition through interfaces for stores and providers, typed domain objects and isolated services. The main maintainability risk is duplicated compatibility logic across migrations and persistence models.'), P('The recommended refactoring is a staged canonical-schema migration, not a rewrite. Add canonical columns, backfill, validate, dual-write temporarily, switch reads, then remove obsolete columns and restore foreign keys.'), P('This preserves the working application while reducing the number of hidden database behaviors.'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('The project source is suitable for academic explanation because each security and cryptographic responsibility can be mapped to a small set of classes and tests. A viva or review can demonstrate the document digest, signed credential, commitment, proof, verification result and audit event as separate steps.'), P('The final source-code chapter should include selected excerpts from the actual repository rather than entire generated files. The most useful excerpts are module declarations, security route rules, digest/signature service interfaces, proof request types, migration definitions and API client behavior.'), Box(5.7*inch,2.0*inch,'FIGURE 4.3 - SOURCE CODE MODULE MAP'), P('Figure 4.3  Source code module map'))
page(H1('4.1 SOURCE CODE'), P('The implementation is complete enough to demonstrate the core concept, but the final submission should distinguish current code from future production work. In particular, filesystem key custody, local rate limiting, database migration compatibility and artifact storage must not be described as solved enterprise features.'), P('The implementation chapter therefore records both the working design and its boundaries. This is important for technical credibility and prevents the report from claiming capabilities that are only planned.'), P('The next chapter evaluates the resulting system using tests, security checks, performance measurements and operational observations.'))

# Chapter 5, pages 47-57
page(CH('CHAPTER 5\nRESULT AND DISCUSSION'), H1('RESULT AND DISCUSSION'), P('The project produces a working privacy-preserving verification workflow. The backend modules compile as a Maven reactor, the frontend builds into static assets, and the application can be packaged as a Spring Boot executable jar. Demo scripts and fixtures support repeatable academic demonstration.'), P('The most important result is the integration of document integrity and predicate verification. A document can be hashed and signed, a credential can bind attributes to an issuer, and a holder can present a proof for a supported statement without returning the raw value.'), P('The result is best classified as high-fidelity prototype and controlled staging software rather than unqualified public production software.'))
page(H1('RESULT AND DISCUSSION'), P('The issuer workflow demonstrates the complete creation path: upload, content validation, digest, artifact storage, credential construction and signature. The verifier workflow demonstrates the reverse path: capability lookup, status and expiry checks, digest or signature verification and clear result semantics.'), P('The holder workflow demonstrates selective disclosure by proving a predicate against a commitment. The proof does not replace credential status or issuer trust checks; it complements them.'), P('The administrator workflow demonstrates key registry lifecycle, audit-chain inspection and recovery-related operations.'))
page(H1('RESULT AND DISCUSSION'), P('The security result is layered rather than dependent on one mechanism. JWT authentication identifies the caller, role checks limit capabilities, ownership checks protect objects, validation limits input, rate limiting reduces abuse, cryptographic verification protects integrity and audit events provide traceability.'), P('The application also uses CSP, frame denial, content-type protection and a strict referrer policy. The container runs as a non-root user with reduced capabilities and a read-only root filesystem.'), P('These controls are appropriate for the project scope but require a real HTTPS edge and external identity provider for public deployment.'))
page(H1('RESULT AND DISCUSSION'), P('The project profile reports strong automated test coverage across backend and frontend. A fresh environment must reproduce those numbers before they are used as final evidence. The release record should include the commit SHA, Java version, Node version, dependency installation result, test count, skipped tests and failure details.'), P('Integration testing against PostgreSQL is particularly important because local SQLite behavior cannot prove PostgreSQL constraint, locking and migration behavior. Testcontainers or a disposable Compose database should be part of the release gate.'), P('A green unit-test result is necessary but not sufficient for a production decision.'))
page(H1('RESULT AND DISCUSSION'), P('The reported cryptographic measurements show that SHA-256 and RSA verification are comparatively inexpensive. RSA-PSS signing is suitable for pilot-scale credential issuance. Pedersen commitment generation is also small relative to proof generation.'), P('Bulletproof generation and verification are the dominant compute operations. The application therefore limits concurrent proof operations and applies endpoint-specific rate limiting. These controls protect the API on a small CPU budget.'), P('The numbers are machine-dependent and must be remeasured on the target host with end-to-end workflows.'))
page(H1('RESULT AND DISCUSSION'), P('The database result is mixed. The schema contains useful lifecycle, ownership, capability and audit structures, but compatibility migrations drop foreign keys and mirror legacy columns through triggers. This makes current startup compatibility easier while weakening database-enforced correctness.'), P('The recommended result for the next release is a canonical PostgreSQL schema with validated foreign keys, explicit uniqueness and transaction boundaries around issuance, revocation, capability creation and audit append.'), P('SQLite should remain a local convenience rather than a production equivalence claim.'))
page(H1('RESULT AND DISCUSSION'), P('The deployment result is a hardened container baseline. The Dockerfile uses Java 21 runtime images, a non-root user, a read-only root filesystem, temporary filesystems, health checks and bounded resources. Compose separates frontend and backend network roles.'), P('The deployment is incomplete for public use because the repository does not include a TLS reverse proxy or managed certificate configuration. The API should not be exposed directly on port 8080 to the internet.'), P('Backup scripts are useful, but a backup strategy is only production-ready after a restore drill proves that database metadata and artifact bytes recover together.'))
page(H1('RESULT AND DISCUSSION'), P('The frontend result is a structured role-oriented application with public verification, issuer upload, holder credential and administrator routes. Error boundaries, authorization guards, loading states and normalized API errors are present in the source.'), P('The final image set should document the visual result at desktop and mobile widths. Screenshots should show the issuer upload state, verification success and failure states, holder proof generation state and administrator audit view.'), Box(5.7*inch,2.1*inch,'FIGURE 5.1 - RESULT SCREENSHOT'), P('Figure 5.1  Result screenshot placeholder'))
page(H1('RESULT AND DISCUSSION'), P('The platform is appropriate for the stated traffic envelope of approximately 5 to 50 concurrent active users, 1,000 to 10,000 requests per day and a few hundred uploads per day, provided the deployment is a single controlled instance with PostgreSQL and bounded proof concurrency.'), P('The current architecture should not be described as horizontally scalable without changes to artifact storage, rate limiting, audit sequencing and key state. Scaling should be triggered by measured demand, not by adding infrastructure for its own sake.'), P('At this stage, simplicity improves reliability.'))
page(H1('RESULT AND DISCUSSION'), P('The primary risks are unsafe configuration defaults, filesystem key custody, local artifact storage, database constraint relaxation, incomplete edge security and unreproducible test evidence. Each risk has a practical mitigation that can be implemented without changing the core product concept.'), P('The project has a strong foundation for improvement because the risky areas are already isolated behind configuration, stores, providers, security filters and migration files. The remediation path is therefore incremental.'), P('A controlled pilot can proceed after fail-closed configuration, clean testing, TLS, backup restore and PostgreSQL validation are demonstrated.'))
page(H1('RESULT AND DISCUSSION'), P('The most important product quality is explainability of verification. Users should see which facts were checked and which were not. A valid proof does not automatically mean that a document is active, an issuer is trusted or a capability is unexpired.'), P('The result screen should present independent status rows for capability, document digest, credential signature, issuer trust, lifecycle and predicate. This is both a UX improvement and a security control because it reduces false confidence.'), Box(5.7*inch,2.1*inch,'FIGURE 5.2 - VERIFICATION RESULT BREAKDOWN'), P('Figure 5.2  Verification result breakdown'))
page(H1('RESULT AND DISCUSSION'), P('The final assessment is conditional approval for academic demonstration and controlled staging. It is not approval for unrestricted public deployment until the production profile, test evidence, PostgreSQL integrity, key custody, TLS edge and backup restore requirements are completed.'), P('This conclusion is deliberately conservative because the project handles documents, credentials and cryptographic trust. Reliability and honest scope are more valuable than an inflated production claim.'), P('The next chapter records the conclusion and future enhancement path.'))

# Chapter 6, pages 58-62
page(CH('CHAPTER 6\nCONCLUSION AND FUTURE ENHANCEMENT'), H1('CONCLUSION'), P('The Zero-Knowledge Document Verification Platform demonstrates how a full-stack Java application can combine document integrity, digital signatures, verifiable credentials and zero-knowledge predicate proofs. The modular architecture separates cryptographic primitives, domain services, persistence, security and UI workflows.'), P('The project is technically significant because it connects mathematical privacy mechanisms to practical issuer, holder and verifier workflows. Its strongest engineering decision is to keep the cryptographic boundary explicit and the overall deployment simple.'), P('The system is suitable for academic evaluation and a carefully controlled pilot after the identified release gates are satisfied.'))
page(H1('CONCLUSION'), P('The project also shows that cryptographic correctness must be supported by ordinary application controls. Secure defaults, authentication, authorization, data validation, lifecycle state, rate limiting, storage integrity, auditability, testing and deployment hardening are all required for a trustworthy result.'), P('The report has intentionally documented only the technology and behavior present in the project repository. Features such as a general proof language, HSM-backed key custody, multi-node artifact storage and distributed rate limiting remain future work rather than current claims.'), P('This distinction makes the report technically defensible.'))
page(H1('FUTURE ENHANCEMENT'), P('The first enhancement is integration with an external OAuth2/OIDC identity provider using authorization code with PKCE, short-lived access tokens and documented issuer, audience and key-rotation validation.'), P('The second enhancement is stronger signing-key custody using a managed KMS or HSM where the deployment budget permits it. If filesystem keys remain for a pilot, permissions, rotation, backup handling and compromise response must be rigorously documented.'), P('The third enhancement is encrypted object storage for document artifacts with versioning, lifecycle policy and restore verification.'))
page(H1('FUTURE ENHANCEMENT'), P('The database should be consolidated around a canonical PostgreSQL schema. Foreign keys should be restored after data repair, compatibility triggers should be removed, and transaction and idempotency rules should be explicit for issuance, revocation, capability creation and artifact persistence.'), P('When multiple API replicas become necessary, rate limiting should move to a shared or edge-enforced policy, while local concurrency guards remain for CPU protection. Horizontal scaling should follow measured workload evidence.'), P('Tenant identity and organization-scoped uniqueness can be added when the platform serves multiple institutions.'))
page(H1('FUTURE ENHANCEMENT'), P('The frontend can be improved through visual regression tests, keyboard and screen-reader validation, clearer multi-state verification cards, responsive mobile layouts and consistent empty, loading and error states. The UI should communicate cryptographic results without using color alone.'), P('Operational enhancements include centralized error tracking, dashboards, alerts, structured redacted logs, OpenTelemetry traces, automated dependency updates and tested backup restoration.'), P('These improvements preserve the current product concept while increasing reliability and maintainability.'))

